@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-operators Operators
@brief Overloaded operators for expressive test comparisons.

@details
These overloads enable expressive, type-safe test assertions. Operators
such as `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, and `>=` are defined in the dedicated
`micro_test_plus::operators` namespace to avoid conflicts with
application-defined operators.

For standard values, the default operators are used, ensuring correct
comparisons. However, when specialised µTest++ operators are used with
typed operands, failed checks display both actual and expected values,
greatly improving report clarity.

These operators are restricted to the framework types (see
`extension::is_op`). For constant values, this is achieved with provided
literals (for example, `1_i`), whilst dynamic values can be wrapped with
converters such as `mt::to_i{expression}`. When neither operand is of
such a type, the standard operators are selected; they return a plain
`bool`, so a failed check cannot show the operands.

There are no operators for strings and containers: the standard
operators would be selected for `std::string` objects and for containers
of the same type, and operators for `std::string_view` objects would
also replace the standard ones in the application code that follows the
`using namespace` directive. Use `mt::eq()` and `mt::ne()` to compare
strings and containers.

Logical operators (`&&`, `||`, `!`) are also supported and provide enhanced
functionality when used with typed operands, allowing complex test
conditions to be composed naturally and readably.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

ts.test ("Operators", [] (auto& t) {
  using namespace mt::operators;
  using namespace mt::literals;

  t.expect (compute_answer () == 42_i) << "answer is 42 (with literal)";
  t.expect (mt::to_i {compute_answer ()} == 42) << "answer is 42 (with cast)";
  t.expect (compute_answer () != 43_i) << "answer is not 43";
  t.expect (compute_answer () < 43_i) << "answer is < 43";
  t.expect (compute_answer () <= 43_i) << "answer is <= 43";
  t.expect (compute_answer () > 41_i) << "answer is > 41";
  t.expect (compute_answer () >= 42_i) << "answer is >= 42";
});
@endcode

@par Logical Operators

Logical operators provide enhanced reporting when used with typed operands,
allowing complex test conditions to be composed naturally and readably.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

ts.test ("Logical operators", [] (auto& t) {
  using namespace mt::operators;
  using namespace mt::literals;

  t.expect (compute_answer () == 42_i && compute_float () == 42.0_f);
});
@endcode
